course: dictionary
lesson: 12035
title: Chuffed: pleased and proud
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words are informal, so people use them in friendly everyday talk more than in formal writing. Chuffed is one of these. Context helps you find its meaning. «Mia got 10 out of 10 for her spelling, and she was chuffed.» Mia got full marks, so she feels very pleased and proud. That is what chuffed means.
explain: Look at the clue words in «Dad was chuffed to bits when I won the race.» The words to bits make the feeling stronger. Chuffed to bits means extremely pleased, not just a little happy. To check, swap in pleased: the sentence still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Chuffed can also be the past form of chuff, which means to make a puffing sound like a steam train. The sentence tells you which meaning is used. «The little engine chuffed along the track.» A train is making a puffing sound, so this chuffed is about the sound, not a feeling.
fact: Chuffed is an adjective here, and it is informal, so you will hear it in chatty speech more than in formal writing.
fact: «Chuffed» means very pleased and proud.
fact: «Chuffed to bits» means extremely pleased.
fact: Chuffed is used most in British, Irish, Australian and New Zealand speech.
fact: Chuff means to make a puffing sound like a steam train, so «chuffed» can also be its past form.
exercise: «Ellie was chuffed when her drawing won the prize.» What does chuffed mean? | *Very pleased and proud | Cold and wet | Angry about the prize | Very tired after a long day
exercise: «The old steam engine chuffed up the hill.» What does chuffed mean here? | Painted the hill red | *Made a puffing sound like a steam train | Turned round and drove home | Felt very pleased
exercise: Sam says, «I'm chuffed to bits with my test result.» Which words show how strong the feeling is? | result | *to bits | test | Sam
test: «Grandma was chuffed to see us at the station.» What does chuffed mean? | Very cold | Very sleepy | Very cross | *Very pleased
test: Oliver says, «Our team is chuffed about the win.» Which feeling fits best? | *Pleased and proud | Hungry for lunch | Bored in class | Scared of the dark
exam: Ruby asks, «Is chuffed a feeling or a sound?» What is the answer? | It is a kind of food | It is only ever a feeling, never a sound | *It can be both: pride, or the puffing sound of a steam train | It is only ever the puffing sound of a steam train
